preConditionHook:
// "A tight trading range trumps everything" (R22-04): while the last N bars
// are unusually tight, no new entry is worth taking whatever else the chart
// shows. The arithmetic is RangeWidthBelowAtrMultiple's, deliberately: the two
// are kept apart by ROLE, not by formula, on the same precedent as the
// catalog's ConsecutiveCloseExit vs MomentumConsecutiveBars. An Entry filter
// can only veto the pane it sits in, while Brooks's claim is a global override
// across every placement, and only a Switch is global.
//
// DIVERGENCE FROM THE BOOK, recorded rather than hidden: the generator's
// Switch role both blocks entries AND forces an exit on the same side
// (strategyWriter.py `_switchLines`). Brooks wants only the block here -- wait
// for the breakout, do not liquidate. There is no block-without-flat role, so
// this rule exits open positions when the range goes tight. If that is not
// wanted, do not place this rule: place RangeWidthBelowAtrMultiple NEGATED in
// each entry pane with these tight parameters instead, which blocks without
// forcing flat but only pane by pane.
//
// Defaults are V24-44's -- 15 bars, 1.2 x the 20-bar average range.
//
// The window extremes mirror EasyLanguage's Extremes(), which Highest()/
// Lowest() call: seeded at bar 0, scanning bars 1..Length-1, strict
// comparison. Extremes is EL SOURCE, so its comparison carries the EL
// tolerance -- el_gt/el_lt, as in Breakout (the reference).
rangeHigh = high[0];
rangeLow = low[0];
for (int i = 1; i < (int)rangeLookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(high[i], rangeHigh)) rangeHigh = high[i];
    if (el_lt(low[i], rangeLow)) rangeLow = low[i];
}
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_AvgTrueRange is a ROLLING accumulator, not a fresh N-term
// sum: seeded once with a full sum on the study's first calculated bar and
// thereafter carrying S += TrueRange - TrueRange[Length], re-seeding when the
// length changes. AtrProfitTarget is the reference and carries the measurement
// notes; the two details that are load-bearing are reproduced here -- the
// re-seed on a length change (what makes it walkforward safe) and the update
// as TWO statements, because EasyLanguage evaluates S[1] + in - out left to
// right and folding it to S += in - out rounds differently.
if (atrLength != (int)atrLookback) {
    atrSum = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < (int)atrLookback; ++i) {
        double hi = high[i] > close[i + 1] ? high[i] : close[i + 1];
        double lo = low[i] < close[i + 1] ? low[i] : close[i + 1];
        atrSum += hi - lo;
    }
    atrLength = (int)atrLookback;
} else {
    const int out = (int)atrLookback;
    double hiIn = high[0] > close[1] ? high[0] : close[1];
    double loIn = low[0] < close[1] ? low[0] : close[1];
    double hiOut = high[out] > close[out + 1] ? high[out] : close[out + 1];
    double loOut = low[out] < close[out + 1] ? low[out] : close[out + 1];
    atrSum += hiIn - loIn;
    atrSum -= hiOut - loOut;
}
atr = atrSum / (int)atrLookback;
